Amswer:
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public int minMoves(String[] classroom, int energy) {
        int m = classroom.length;
        int n = classroom[0].length();

        int startR = 0;
        int startC = 0;

        List<int[]> litter = new ArrayList<>();

        // Find starting position and litter positions
        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                char ch = classroom[i].charAt(j);

                if (ch == 'S') {
                    startR = i;
                    startC = j;
                } else if (ch == 'L') {
                    litter.add(new int[]{i, j});
                }
            }
        }

        int totalLitter = litter.size();

        if (totalLitter == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int allCollected = (1 << totalLitter) - 1;

        // visited[row][col][energy][mask]
        boolean[][][][] visited =
            new boolean[m][n][energy + 1][1 << totalLitter];

        Queue<int[]> queue = new LinkedList<>();

        queue.offer(new int[]{
            startR, startC, energy, 0
        });

        visited[startR][startC][energy][0] = true;

        int[][] directions = {
            {1, 0},
            {-1, 0},
            {0, 1},
            {0, -1}
        };

        int moves = 0;

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {

            int size = queue.size();

            while (size-- > 0) {

                int[] state = queue.poll();

                int r = state[0];
                int c = state[1];
                int e = state[2];
                int mask = state[3];

                // All litter collected
                if (mask == allCollected) {
                    return moves;
                }

                for (int[] dir : directions) {

                    int nr = r + dir[0];
                    int nc = c + dir[1];

                    // Out of bounds
                    if (nr < 0 || nr >= m || nc < 0 || nc >= n) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    // Cannot walk through obstacle
                    if (classroom[nr].charAt(nc) == 'X') {
                        continue;
                    }

                    // No energy to make a move
                    if (e == 0) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    int newEnergy = e - 1;
                    int newMask = mask;

                    char nextCell = classroom[nr].charAt(nc);

                    // Collect litter
                    if (nextCell == 'L') {
                        for (int i = 0; i < totalLitter; i++) {
                            if (litter.get(i)[0] == nr &&
                                litter.get(i)[1] == nc) {

                                newMask |= (1 << i);
                                break;
                            }
                        }
                    }

                    // Reset energy
                    if (nextCell == 'R') {
                        newEnergy = energy;
                    }

                    if (!visited[nr][nc][newEnergy][newMask]) {

                        visited[nr][nc][newEnergy][newMask] = true;

                        queue.offer(new int[]{
                            nr,
                            nc,
                            newEnergy,
                            newMask
                        });
                    }
                }
            }

            moves++;
        }

        return -1;
    }
}


Complexity:
Time: roughly O(m × n × energy × 2^L × 4) where L <= 10.
Space: O(m × n × energy × 2^L).


Explanation:
Suppose there are 3 litter cells:

L1 L2 L3

We represent collected litter as:

000 → none collected
001 → L1 collected
010 → L2 collected
101 → L1 and L3 collected
111 → all collected

So with 10 litter cells, there are only:

2^10 = 1024

possible masks.

BFS state

We need to remember four things:

(row, col, energy, collectedLitter)

Because reaching the same cell isn't necessarily the same state.

For example:

(2, 3, energy=5, mask=001)

and

(2, 3, energy=1, mask=001)

are different states.

BFS guarantees that the first time we reach a state where all litter is collected, the number of moves is minimum.
